/*****************************************************************************
    litProbe -- SCRATCH MEASUREMENT, 2026-08-16. What does a literal rule term
    look like under the new labelled-literal representation?

    Prints dumpRuleTerms for the three Grokking rules Tony relabelled. The
    UNlabelled counterpart is already captured by incant/countScratch, whose
    Ladder rules (Scaf, Scaf2, ScafA) spell their literals bare.

    THE MEASURED SPLIT, which is what the classifier keys on:
        labelled     leftBrace="["-    tag=leftBrace   data=isSTRING   noLabel CLEAR
        unlabelled   "x"-              tag=GrouP       data=isSTRING   noLabel SET
    In both the spelling has left the TAG and lives in the DATA.

    Reads nothing, changes nothing. Delete when the classifier question closes.

    SENTINEL AT THE FOOT, checked first and by name.

    RUN:   <binary> incant/litProbe
*****************************************************************************/
registry(cOMMANDs);
define dumpRuleTerms immediateAction=dumpRuleTerms; ;

dumpRuleTerms('Braced');
dumpRuleTerms('Parens');
dumpRuleTerms('BlocK');
dumpRuleTerms('FormaT');
dumpRuleTerms('DatA');

/*  COMPLETENESS ASSERTION, and it is NOT a print. `print` at top level in a
    probe with no search preamble produces ZERO BYTES of stdout at exit 0 --
    symptom reproduced 2026-08-16, NOT diagnosed, and the same silent-spelling
    family as bear-trap #28. A stdout sentinel here would assert nothing.
    So the check is the LAST DUMP ITSELF, on stderr where dumpRuleTerms writes:

        RULE BlocK present  <=>  the file reached its final statement
        3 `^RULE ` lines    <=>  nothing in the middle was skipped

    Both are checked by name, and neither can be satisfied by a truncated run.  */
